// ==============================================================================================
// present.fj       drives the screen device: command bytes on the output stream.
// STATUS  LIVE - the generated main
// ENTRY   present.init_screen_stream, present.set_palette, present.begin_frame_collines
// SIZE    14 macros (present.init_screen_stream .. present.emit_cmd_byte)
// ==============================================================================================
// F7 (present layer, M11a) — drive the screen device over the output stream.
// Device protocol (authoritative: flipjump InMemoryScreen / ScreenIO.py): command bytes on the
// output stream; the memory-hook present (0x03) hands the device a framebuffer bit-address and it
// DMA-reads W*H packed bytes (stride dw, row-major) straight from program memory (~free, D3/F7).
// Screen geometry comes from fj_consts (W/H/BPP/NCOLORS — the config SSOT, R6). Input poll is M14.
//
// ---------- ORIENTATION: the command-byte map (this file IS the wire-protocol spec)
// Every macro below is a pure EMITTER: it writes command/payload bytes with stl.output_char and
// touches no state. Each macro's comment is the authoritative byte grammar for the command it
// emits — read the table to find the one you need, then read that macro's comment for the exact
// layout. (0x01 has two macros: the stock 8-byte init and the extended 9-byte stream init.)
//
//   byte  macro                    payload                          used by which renderer tier
//   ----  -----------------------  -------------------------------  --------------------------
//   0x01  init_screen              W,H,BPP,NCOLORS (8 bytes)        legacy framebuffer tier
//   0x01  init_screen_stream       ...+flush_mode (9 bytes)         every stream/device tier
//   0x02  set_palette              addr                             all tiers
//   0x03  update_screen            addr (packed-byte FB)            legacy framebuffer tier
//   0x06  update_screen_reg        addr (hex.vec 2 FB)              legacy framebuffer tier
//   0x07  begin_frame_stream       —                                tests only (no emitter tier)
//   0x09  begin_frame_planes       —                                visplane-band tier
//   0x0A  begin_frame_spans        —                                dumb-screen fillCol tier
//   0x0B  begin_frame_collines     —                                *** THE SHIPPED TIER ***
//   0x0C  load_raster_tables       3 addrs                          raster + proj tiers
//   0x0D  begin_frame_raster       —                                device-rasterizer tier
//   0x0E  load_proj_tables         addr + count:2                   proj tier
//   0x0F  begin_frame_proj         —                                proj tier
//   (0x04/0x05/0x08 are not emitted by this file. `emit_addr` is a helper, not a command.)
//
// ---------- Conventions that hold in this file
// * TIERS. The tiers above are ALTERNATIVES, selected once by the Python emitter
//   (src/doomfj/wall_renderer.py) — a program emits exactly one of them. They are strictly
//   ADDITIVE: the 0x01/0x02/0x03/0x06 stock commands are unchanged and still drive the stock
//   flipjump InMemoryScreen; 0x07 and up are decoded only by the in-repo StreamScreen subclass
//   (tests/fj/stream_screen.py), which the stock device never sees.
// * ORDERING. `load_*_tables` are ONCE-PER-PROGRAM address handoffs (right after set_palette);
//   `begin_frame_*` starts a per-frame stream. ⚠ For tiers that emit records INLINE during the
//   BSP walk (raster/proj/collines), set_palette + the load_* handoffs MUST precede the walk, or
//   their command bytes land inside the record stream and corrupt it.
// * ADDRESSES are handed over, never copied: `emit_addr` writes a w-bit label as w/8
//   little-endian bytes and the device DMA-reads the table/framebuffer straight out of program
//   memory. That is why a table costs ~zero fj ops to "send" (D3/F7).
// * COMPILE-TIME ONLY. `stl.output_char` needs a constant operand, so every parameter here
//   (addresses, counts, flush_mode) is a compile-time value — none may be a runtime register.
// * IN-STREAM TAGS, not command bytes — in the TAGGED formats only. 0x0A/0x0B/0x0D end with a
//   single 0xFF terminator; 0x0B also tags 0xFE = ditto and 0x0D tags 0xFE/0xFD = ceil/floor
//   visplane records; in those three the tag values can never collide with a valid first payload
//   byte. 0x0F ends with the two-byte [0xFF][0xFF], discriminated by the SECOND byte (a seg id's
//   lo byte may be 0xFF; its hi byte never is). The LENGTH-DRIVEN formats (0x07 run stream,
//   0x09 planes) have NO terminator — the device counts pixels/records. 0x07 additionally has no
//   reserved byte values at all (0xFF/0xFE are ordinary [count]/[color] bytes); 0x09 reserves
//   only 0xFF, and only in a column record's `fvp` field (= unclaimed, paints nothing).
// * The decoder for each frame format lives in tests/fj/stream_screen.py and is named in the
//   macro's comment — that pair (macro comment + decoder) is the protocol's two-sided SSOT.

ns present {
    // emit a w-bit label/address as w/8 little-endian bytes (includes zero bytes, unlike stl.output).
    def emit_addr addr {
        rep(w/8, i) stl.output_char (addr >> (8*i)) & 0xff
    }

    // init_screen: [0x01][W:2][H:2][BPP:1][palette_entries=NCOLORS:2]
    def init_screen {
        stl.output_char 0x01
        stl.output_char W & 0xff
        stl.output_char (W >> 8) & 0xff
        stl.output_char H & 0xff
        stl.output_char (H >> 8) & 0xff
        stl.output_char BPP
        stl.output_char NCOLORS & 0xff
        stl.output_char (NCOLORS >> 8) & 0xff
    }

    // set_palette: [0x02][palette_addr: w/8] — `addr` is the packed-byte palette base (3 bytes/entry).
    def set_palette addr {
        stl.output_char 0x02
        .emit_addr addr
    }

    // update_screen (present): [0x03][framebuffer_addr: w/8] — the device reads the finished frame.

    // update_screen_reg (present, REGISTER form, fj 1.5.1): [0x06][framebuffer_addr: w/8] — the device
    // reads a `hex.vec 2` framebuffer (each pixel = two ops: low nibble at 2k, high at 2k+1, bpp 8).
    // This is the format a colormap/texture `.lookup` writes DIRECTLY (its result-copy is register-form),
    // so the full-unroll renderer (M11c, D2b) has NO deposit/pack step (the hex.vec2 simplification,
    // §2.1). The framebuffer label must be declared `hex.vec 2*FB_SIZE`.
    def update_screen_reg addr {
        stl.output_char 0x06
        .emit_addr addr
    }

    // ===== M13pS0 and later: the column-stream protocol. Owned by this repo and NOT frozen — it
    // may be evolved, but only ADDITIVELY (everything above this line is the stock device's and
    // must stay byte-for-byte as it is). Decoded by StreamScreen(InMemoryScreen) in
    // tests/fj/stream_screen.py, which expects the EXTENDED 9-byte init; the stock InMemoryScreen
    // sees neither that nor any command byte from here down.
    // (history: docs/m13p-procedural-plan.md)

    // init_screen_stream: [0x01][W:2][H:2][BPP:1][NCOLORS:2][flush_mode:1]. `flush_mode` is a
    // COMPILE-TIME constant (0 or 1, decided once by the Python emitter, never per-frame) — 0 = flush
    // the device's presented frame once per FULL FRAME (after column VIEW_W-1 fills; the shipped
    // default), 1 = flush once per COLUMN (debugging/streaming a partial frame). `stl.output_char`
    // requires a compile-time operand, so `flush_mode` cannot be a runtime register.
    def init_screen_stream flush_mode {
        stl.output_char 0x01
        stl.output_char W & 0xff
        stl.output_char (W >> 8) & 0xff
        stl.output_char H & 0xff
        stl.output_char (H >> 8) & 0xff
        stl.output_char BPP
        stl.output_char NCOLORS & 0xff
        stl.output_char (NCOLORS >> 8) & 0xff
        stl.output_char flush_mode & 0xff
    }

    // begin_frame_stream: [0x07] — resets the device's write cursor to (column 0, row 0). Every byte
    // that follows, until exactly VIEW_W*VIEW_H pixels have been placed, is consumed as the raw
    // column-major run stream `[count:1][color:1]` (no per-run command-byte framing — the device
    // knows the total pixel count from init_screen_stream, so the last run naturally completes the
    // frame with no explicit end marker). `byte.emit`/`cm.emit`-style EMIT tables are what actually
    // produce those bytes (see doomfj.lut_generator.generate_emit_dispatch_table_fj). No Python
    // emitter tier uses this command today — hand-written fj tests only. Decode:
    // stream_screen.py::_handle_stream_byte.
    def begin_frame_stream {
        stl.output_char 0x07
    }

    // begin_frame_planes: [0x09] (M13-planesproto) — the whole frame arrives as SHARED per-visplane
    // band lists + one 5-byte record per column ([cexcl][fstart][lit][cvp][fvp]; fvp=0xFF =
    // unclaimed, paints nothing); the device does the per-column prefix/suffix clipping that
    // stream.emit_column used to pay fj ops for. Payload layout + exact column semantics:
    // tests/fj/stream_screen.py::_decode_planes_frame (upstream with the 0x01/0x07 extension).

    // begin_frame_spans: [0x0A] (M13-spanfill) — the DUMB-SCREEN protocol. The whole frame arrives as
    // a flat list of explicit fillCol records [x:1][y1:1][y2:1][colour:1], terminated by a sentinel
    // record whose x == 0xFF. The device does NOTHING but fill: for each record it sets column `x`,
    // rows [y1, y2), to palette index `colour` — no cursor state, no band lists, no clipping, no
    // lookups. fj does all the geometry/lighting/clipping and hands the device only straight coloured
    // vertical strips. (A horizontal-span twin — fillRow [x1][x2][y][colour] — is the natural
    // extension for a row-major floor pass; not emitted yet.) Decode: stream_screen.py::_handle_spans_byte.

    // M13-lines3 — begin_frame_collines: [0x0B]. The PACKED form of the 0x0A dumb-screen frame:
    // the device still only prints what it's told, but the wire format drops the redundant per-
    // record x/y1. Records, tagged by their first byte:
    //   x < 0xF0: a COLUMN RUN-LIST -- [x], then pairs [y2:1][colour:1] filling rows [prev,y2)
    //             top-down starting at prev=0, terminated by a single [0xFF] (y2 is <= VIEW_H=100
    //             for real pairs, so 0xFF is unambiguous). If the byte after [x] is [0xFE], the
    //             record is a DITTO instead: copy column x-1's pixels into column x, record done.
    //             Emitted when a column's output would be byte-identical to column x-1's — inline
    //             by the walk leaf in the shipped tier (frame_render.fj's emit_ditto path), by
    //             stream_render.fj::flush_frame in the rung-3b mode; never for column 0.
    //   0xFF:     end of frame -> present.
    // No clipping, no lookups, no decisions device-side: every row range and colour was computed
    // in fj. Decode: stream_screen.py::_handle_collines_byte.
    def begin_frame_collines {
        stl.output_char 0x0B
    }

    // M13-raster — the DEVICE RASTERIZER. fj stays the "brain" (BSP walk, visibility/occlusion
    // DECISIONS, per-seg projection setup); the device does pure rendering MECHANICS from compact
    // records: a wall-column DDA (2 adds/column: scale+=scalestep, then a fixed_mul+shift for
    // top/bottom), a first-writer-wins fill, and a distance-light shade (a threshold march +
    // table lookups — the SAME arithmetic `plane.build_bands`/`_zidx_band_walk` already do, just
    // executed device-side instead of costing fj ops). No game state, no visibility decisions: the
    // walk's front-to-back EMIT ORDER is what encodes occlusion; the device only refuses to
    // overwrite an already-painted column.

    // load_raster_tables: [0x0C][yslope_addr:w/8][zlight_addr:w/8][colormap_addr:w/8] — ONCE per
    // program (not per frame, right after set_palette): hands the device the addresses of three
    // STATIC fj-baked tables it DMA-reads directly (zero fj ops, exactly like set_palette/
    // update_screen's existing address-handoff DMA). yslope_addr: packed-byte, 3 bytes/row,
    // VIEW_H rows (the SAME table `plane.build_bands` used, see generate_yslope_packed_lut_fj).
    // zlight_addr: packed-byte, 2 bytes/entry (wait: nibble-vec 2, 1 byte via read_table), the
    // LIGHTLEVELS*MAXLIGHTZ flattened distance-light grid (generate_zlight_lut_fj). colormap_addr:
    // packed-byte, COLORMAP_LIGHTS*256 raw palette-index bytes, ROW-MAJOR [light_row][texel]
    // (generate_colormap_table_fj — NEW, a raw addressable twin of the flattened cm.emit dispatch).
    def load_raster_tables yslope_addr, zlight_addr, colormap_addr {
        stl.output_char 0x0C
        .emit_addr yslope_addr
        .emit_addr zlight_addr
        .emit_addr colormap_addr
    }

    // begin_frame_raster: [0x0D] — starts the per-frame raster stream. What follows (interleaved,
    // in BSP front-to-back walk order — no separate "buffer then emit" phase needed since ordering
    // alone encodes occlusion) is: per newly-referenced visplane, ONE 8-byte record
    // [tag:1][vp_idx:1][planeheight:4][light:1][base:1] with tag 0xFE = ceiling visplane,
    // 0xFD = floor visplane; per surviving seg, ONE record
    // [x1:1][x2:1][scale1:4][scalestep:4][wt_ceil:4][wt_floor:4][lit:1][cvp_idx:1][fvp_idx:1]
    // (21 bytes; x1 < 0xFD always, so the device tells records apart by their first byte). The
    // stream ends with ONE terminator byte 0xFF (never a valid x1 or tag). Decode:
    // stream_screen.py::_handle_raster_byte + _decode_raster_record.

    // M13-proj (Path B, lab mode): [0x0E][seg_geom addr][count:2] — hand the device the STATIC
    // per-one-sided-seg geometry table (packed bytes, 30 B/row: v1x,v1y,v2x,v2y,segangle each 2 B,
    // a,b,c each 4 B, ceil_h,floor_h each 2 B, light,lit,ceilbase,floorbase each 1 B) ONCE per
    // program, DMA-read like load_raster_tables' three. With the geometry resident device-side, the
    // per-frame seg record collapses to a 2-byte compact seg id.

    // begin_frame_proj: [0x0F] — the vertex->column PROJECTION frame (Path B). Framing after the
    // command byte is positional (no tags): [viewx_map:2 signed][viewy_map:2 signed][viewangle:4]
    // then [viewz:4] (emitted by the walk's player-subsector block, always before any seg id), then
    // 2-byte little-endian compact seg ids in BSP front-to-back walk order (fj has already applied
    // the wedge + affine back-face culls; the device does wall_x_range/scale/DDA/fill/shade from the
    // resident geometry, first-writer-wins per column), terminated by [0xFF][0xFF] (id hi byte is
    // never 0xFF: counts are < 0xFF00 segs). Decode: stream_screen.py::_handle_proj_byte.
}
